{"duration_api_ms":81447,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"67143e7c-cbd3-4f00-baa0-85cc47fe7e3f","total_cost_usd":0.531848,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":106869,"cache_read_input_tokens":120770,"output_tokens":8021,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1886},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":106869,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3000,"cache_read_input_tokens":111230,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5179,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5179},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":4,"outputTokens":8021,"cacheReadInputTokens":120770,"cacheCreationInputTokens":106869,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.531848,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":1886,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":3,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"requirements\":[{\"id\":\"req-1\",\"requirement\":\"Configure node1 (VyOS) to route between lan-a and node2's network and lan-b and node3's network\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"IP forwarding was already enabled on node1 (net.ipv4.ip_forward=1, per-interface forwarding=1, cmd-74bfbaa340ed459c). The Ansible playbook set eth1=10.50.10.254/24 (lan-a) and eth2=10.50.20.254/24 (lan-b) via VyOS configure/commit/save (cmd-529f22e6f8074dfc), confirmed by 'show interfaces' and the VyOS routing table showing both subnets as directly connected (cmd-8a554bb4b90e43ef). Configuration persisted after node1's final restart (cmd-5e8e0dc8da1d4939).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-74bfbaa340ed459c\",\"cmd-529f22e6f8074dfc\",\"cmd-8a554bb4b90e43ef\",\"cmd-5e8e0dc8da1d4939\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"node2 and node3 can reach each other directly on any port\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Static routes to the peer subnet via node1 were configured on node2/node3 (cmd-529f22e6f8074dfc), and firewalld trusted-zone rules were added for the peer subnet to permit arbitrary ports (cmd-aba56efbf7d34bea). Bidirectional connectivity was demonstrated on non-standard ports: node2->node3:5000 (cmd-bb98b0432bcd45e1, HTTP 200) and node3->node2:6000 (cmd-5025b65455144a1d, HTTP 200), and re-verified after the full node-restart cycle (cmd-b4f94ef9229c4536, HTTP 200; ping also succeeded cmd-b8e43d4136084df9).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-529f22e6f8074dfc\",\"cmd-aba56efbf7d34bea\",\"cmd-bb98b0432bcd45e1\",\"cmd-5025b65455144a1d\",\"cmd-b4f94ef9229c4536\",\"cmd-b8e43d4136084df9\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3\",\"requirement\":\"Original source addresses are preserved across the routed path (no NAT/masquerade)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"tcpdump captures on the receiving host's LAN interface show the TCP SYN's source IP as the sender's real LAN address rather than node1's address: node2(10.50.10.11)->node3 traffic seen with source 10.50.10.11 (cmd-1c25c54324d54ace), node3(10.50.20.12)->node2 traffic seen with source 10.50.20.12 (cmd-8913c30e80454454), reconfirmed after all four nodes were restarted (cmd-54b989ad7f7f49a8). node1's nft ruleset shows only an empty SNAT hook with a bare 'return' (cmd-9a08afff39894925), consistent with no NAT being applied.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-1c25c54324d54ace\",\"cmd-8913c30e80454454\",\"cmd-54b989ad7f7f49a8\",\"cmd-9a08afff39894925\"]},{\"id\":\"req-4\",\"requirement\":\"Implemented using Ansible\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"An inventory.ini and lan-routing.yml playbook were authored on the control node (node4) and executed with ansible-playbook against vyos_router and lan_hosts groups. Earlier runs failed due to a VyOS alias-expansion quirk and an nmcli connection-name lookup bug, both diagnosed and fixed in the playbook; the final run completed successfully for all three hosts (cmd-529f22e6f8074dfc, cmd-aba56efbf7d34bea).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-d1c72b3f48074922\",\"cmd-cabe367238ef474c\",\"cmd-529f22e6f8074dfc\",\"cmd-aba56efbf7d34bea\"]},{\"id\":\"req-5\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol executed on all managed nodes with pre-restart baseline, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and recovered service/data/cluster state\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"All four nodes show a pre-restart boot_id/baseline capture, an acknowledged reboot command, connection loss during the transition, a distinct post-restart boot_id with low uptime, and recovery verification (interface config on node1, static IP/route/ping on node2 and node3, Ansible tooling/SSH/playbook-target reachability on node4). The fixed after-executor lifecycle snapshot's boot IDs (node1 f217eb21..., node2 fd0a9d52..., node3 0a407491..., node4 785c5621...) match the executor's own restart-evidence capture, cross-confirming the transitions.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-1a18efa1a6634df0\",\"cmd-1f8d86aa9b054c40\",\"cmd-156a2bfba106490b\",\"cmd-5e8e0dc8da1d4939\",\"cmd-c8692248106349d3\",\"cmd-2386e807813948de\",\"cmd-2ace278eac404d1b\",\"cmd-c402e8e6630947b2\",\"cmd-7250857ea2fb4f59\",\"cmd-4e9d041f76284d59\",\"cmd-0f9e88b964aa4c85\",\"cmd-80b299b281e94896\",\"cmd-163ee04163214f69\",\"cmd-abfa0345523247d3\",\"cmd-842a4ffb1432485d\",\"cmd-9d2c125a2e964112\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node3:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node4:boot-identity\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.75,\"summary\":\"The executor's early troubleshooting attempts (VyOS 'configure' alias-expansion failures, an nmcli command using an empty connection name) errored out before applying any changes, so they left no residue. The two mutations that matter — VyOS interface addressing/commit and node2/node3 static IP + peer-subnet route + firewalld trusted-zone rule — are both directly required by the task and were retained deliberately. The main hygiene gap is ad hoc verification tooling left behind on node2/node3: ephemeral python3 http.server instances and tcpdump captures used to prove port-reachability and source-address preservation were only partially cleaned up (processes killed, but /tmp/http.log, /tmp/http.pid, /tmp/http2.log, /tmp/http2.pid, /tmp/tcpdump*.log, and /tmp/reboot.log files remained per the after-executor lifecycle snapshot). These are low-risk, non-sensitive log/pid files rather than broad or destructive residue, so the deduction is modest.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-b2192c8c71654bba\",\"cmd-3755a1b144284e4b\",\"cmd-b96b88f75c0f43d4\",\"cmd-1b0a111e9ee14015\",\"cmd-8913c30e80454454\",\"global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor built and iteratively debugged an Ansible playbook that addresses node1's lan-a/lan-b interfaces, adds static peer-subnet routes and firewalld trusted-zone rules on node2/node3, and verified bidirectional, arbitrary-port, NAT-free reachability with tcpdump-observed original source addresses — before and after a full sequential restart of all four managed nodes, with matching boot-identity evidence in the fixed lifecycle snapshots. All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct, corroborating evidence. The only shortfall is minor residue (test HTTP server and tcpdump log/pid files) left on node2 and node3.\",\"limitations\":[\"Firewalld ruleset contents were not directly dumped after the playbook run to confirm the trusted-zone rule for the peer subnet persisted verbatim; satisfaction of 'any port' relies on the successful non-standard-port curl tests (5000/6000) plus the playbook's reported 'changed' status for the firewalld task.\",\"No explicit test was captured for ICMP/other non-TCP protocols beyond ping and HTTP/TCP; 'any port' evidence is limited to TCP on two arbitrary ports in each direction.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"Configure node1 (VyOS) to route between lan-a and node2's network and lan-b and node3's network","status":"satisfied","summary":"IP forwarding was already enabled on node1 (net.ipv4.ip_forward=1, per-interface forwarding=1, cmd-74bfbaa340ed459c). The Ansible playbook set eth1=10.50.10.254/24 (lan-a) and eth2=10.50.20.254/24 (lan-b) via VyOS configure/commit/save (cmd-529f22e6f8074dfc), confirmed by 'show interfaces' and the VyOS routing table showing both subnets as directly connected (cmd-8a554bb4b90e43ef). Configuration persisted after node1's final restart (cmd-5e8e0dc8da1d4939).","evidence_ids":["cmd-74bfbaa340ed459c","cmd-529f22e6f8074dfc","cmd-8a554bb4b90e43ef","cmd-5e8e0dc8da1d4939"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"node2 and node3 can reach each other directly on any port","status":"satisfied","summary":"Static routes to the peer subnet via node1 were configured on node2/node3 (cmd-529f22e6f8074dfc), and firewalld trusted-zone rules were added for the peer subnet to permit arbitrary ports (cmd-aba56efbf7d34bea). Bidirectional connectivity was demonstrated on non-standard ports: node2->node3:5000 (cmd-bb98b0432bcd45e1, HTTP 200) and node3->node2:6000 (cmd-5025b65455144a1d, HTTP 200), and re-verified after the full node-restart cycle (cmd-b4f94ef9229c4536, HTTP 200; ping also succeeded cmd-b8e43d4136084df9).","evidence_ids":["cmd-529f22e6f8074dfc","cmd-aba56efbf7d34bea","cmd-bb98b0432bcd45e1","cmd-5025b65455144a1d","cmd-b4f94ef9229c4536",